<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Bug: double-clicking a "blueish" container clip in the arranger did nothing
  for several clips. `scutrndrinline.cpp`'s cutIsContainer() paints BLUE any
  audio content with no random source (a registered arrangement, a take
  stack, a plain folder-track window, a nested SCut) — but the old
  mouseDoubleClickEvent only ever asked arrangementNameOf(), the narrowest of
  those. Everything else painted blue and did nothing on a double-click.

  THE RULE, implemented in SMVActualView::tryOpenContainerClip():
    1. content is a REGISTERED ARRANGEMENT  -> open/front its tab (unchanged)
    2. content is a TAKE STACK               -> show that clip's track's
                                                 take lanes (never toggle
                                                 closed — SHOW, not toggle)
    3. content is a plain TRACK with children -> reveal that track's own
                                                 lanes (expand every
                                                 collapsed ancestor)
    4. no clip at all, on a FOLDER LANE       -> toggle that folder's fold
                                                 (double-click-lane, the
                                                 only route to this — there
                                                 is no clip to address)
    5. anything else still blue               -> try the tab route; if it
                                                 refuses, report it (status
                                                 bar + TW_LOG), never silence

  A take column is unwrapped FIRST (SLink -> STakeStack directly, as
  add-take produces at runtime): the ACTIVE take is resolved and the rule
  above re-applied to IT, so a container cut sitting in a take column
  behaves exactly like the same cut placed on its own (rule table case 3 of
  the bug report). Section 4 below is the gate for that unwrap.

  THE EXACT LEGACY SHAPE behind 5 of the 7 blue clips in the reported
  project — an SCut whose CONTENT is an STakeStack (as opposed to an SLink
  pointing straight at one) — predates proposal 37 D8b's window-typing of
  STakeStack and is loadable but NOT reachable through any VERB this suite
  has (add-take always produces the direct SLink->STakeStack shape). Section
  2 below exercises the SAME resolve branch (content resolves to a bare
  STakeStack with no active take, which "show the take lanes" covers
  identically) through the shape a script CAN build: a stack deactivated via
  select-take.

  THAT WAS NOT THE WHOLE STORY. Showing take lanes for the legacy shape
  turned out to do nothing OBSERVABLE: proposal 17's take-lane row/paint/
  gesture code (main/timeline/CONTRACT.md inv. 23's follow-up paragraph)
  resolved a clip's take stack with a bare `dynamic_cast<STakeStack*>` that
  only matches the direct shape, so the legacy one got no row built and
  nothing painted even once "shown". That is gated separately, on the
  legacy shape itself (a hand-written fixture, since no verb builds it):
  `qxa.takestack_legacy_wrap_lanes`.

  Also not gated here: pixel exactness of the fold triangle/tab UI, and real
  mouse ergonomics (drag, hover) — every gesture here is a synthesized
  press/release/dblclick/release, same as every other clip gesture in this
  suite.

  THE ROW-NUMBER TRICK. assert-lane-overlay's `row=N` is read off the MODEL
  layout (rowIndexOfTrack), not off pixels, so it is a real report whatever
  the grab measures, which is what folder_sum_preview.qxa's own negative
  controls (expectOverlay="false") lean on. Here the sentinel tracks carry no
  material of their own, so their band can show incidental non-fill pixels
  (a leftover selection tint, a playhead sample that misses the colour-
  identity check) that have nothing to do with this feature; asserting
  expectOverlay="false" against that noise asserts something this case does
  not care about. expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" sidesteps it without a
  new verb: minPixels=0 makes the floor check pass by construction (overlay
  can never be negative), so only fillPx>0 (any lane has SOME fill colour)
  and the contains="row=N" check are load-bearing.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="doubleclick_blue_clip_resolve">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- The grid crosses every lane and would land in the overlay pixel
         histogram as chrome (folder_sum_preview.qxa's own reasoning). -->
    <grid-disable/>

    <!-- ================================================================
         SECTION 1 (rule 1): a registered-arrangement asset clip STILL
         opens a tab exactly as before. Full regression coverage of this
         path already lives in tabs_doubleclick_drillin.qxa; this is a
         smoke check that the new resolve did not disturb it.
         ================================================================ -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 0: leaf, own clip -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 1: extracted -->
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="1" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <extract-arrangement trackPaths="1" rangeStart="0" rangeEnd="192000"
                         name="Drums" window="range" placeAt="0"/>
    <close-arrangement-tab name="Drums"/>
    <assert-tab-set names="Arrangement" active="Arrangement"/>
    <assert-track-name trackPath="1" name="Drums"/>

    <double-click-clip track="1" clip="0"/>
    <assert-tab-set names="Arrangement,Drums" active="Drums"/>
    <activate-tab name="Arrangement"/>

    <!-- ================================================================
         SECTION 2 (rule 2): a TAKE STACK clip shows its take lanes.
         nTakes()==2, activeTake==-1 (a legitimate STakeStack state:
         "activeTake_ in [-1, nTakes())") is what makes the clip resolve
         to the STAKE STACK ITSELF rather than to one take's own content,
         so this is the live, verb-reachable way to exercise rule 2's
         branch. Observed the folder_sum_preview way: through a SENTINEL
         lane's row number moving, never a row-count probe.
         ================================================================ -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 2: take-stack track -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 3: sentinel -->
    <add-sample trackIndex="2" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <add-take clip="2,0" filePath="../test_stereo.wav" startOffset="0"
             activate="0"/>
    <select-take clip="2,0" take="-1" broadcast="0"/>

    <!-- Before: rows 0..3, one per track, sentinel at row 3. -->
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="3" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=3"/>

    <double-click-clip track="2" clip="0"/>

    <!-- After: two take-lane rows appear under track 2's own lane, pushing
         the sentinel from row 3 to row 5. -->
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="3" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=5"/>

    <!-- A second double-click must NOT close what the first one opened
         (user decision: SHOW, never toggle-closed). -->
    <double-click-clip track="2" clip="0"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="3" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=5"/>

    <!-- Collapse the take lanes back so every row number after this point
         stays simple to reason about. -->
    <set-lane-view toggleTakesRow="2"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="3" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=3"/>

    <!-- ================================================================
         SECTION 3 (rule 3): a clip whose content is a plain TRACK (what
         create-asset over a FOLDER TRACK produces) reveals that track's
         own lanes.
         ================================================================ -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 4: folder -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 5: (temp) child -->
    <add-sample trackIndex="5" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <reparent-track source="5" destParent="4" destIndex="-1"/>
    <!-- Top level now: 0,1(Drums),2,3,4(folder with 1 child). -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 5: asset target -->

    <create-asset container="4" startOffset="0" duration="192000"
                  assetName="FolderAsset"/>
    <place-asset assetName="FolderAsset" trackPath="5" timePos="0"/>

    <collapse-track trackPath="4" collapsed="1"/>
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="4" collapsed="1"/>

    <double-click-clip track="5" clip="0"/>
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="4" collapsed="0"/>

    <!-- Reset, so Section 5's row arithmetic starts from a clean baseline
         (one visible row per top-level track). -->
    <collapse-track trackPath="4" collapsed="1"/>
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="4" collapsed="1"/>

    <!-- ================================================================
         SECTION 4 (the case-3 unwrap fix): a take stack placed directly
         on the track (SLink -> STakeStack, what add-take builds) whose
         ACTIVE take is itself a container cut over a REGISTERED
         ARRANGEMENT. Before the fix, dynamic_cast<SCut*> on the STACK
         itself failed outright and this fell all the way through to
         nothing; the unwrap-then-resolve makes it drill in exactly as a
         bare asset clip would.
         ================================================================ -->
    <create-arrangement name="Drums2"/>
    <create-asset container="Drums2:" startOffset="0" duration="192000"
                  assetName="Drums2Asset"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 6: target -->
    <place-asset assetName="Drums2Asset" trackPath="6" timePos="0"/>
    <!-- Wrap it into a 2-take stack; activate="0" keeps the ORIGINAL
         (Drums2-windowing) take active, matching "add-take onto an
         existing clip keeps the wrapped material audible". -->
    <add-take clip="6,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="0"
             activate="0"/>

    <double-click-clip track="6" clip="0"/>
    <assert-tab-set names="Arrangement,Drums,Drums2" active="Drums2"/>
    <activate-tab name="Arrangement"/>

    <!-- ================================================================
         SECTION 5 (rule 4 / "case 4"): a bare FOLDER LANE — no clip of
         the track's own — toggles the fold, exactly what the head's fold
         triangle does. This is the literal "I expected the lanes to open"
         report. double-click-clip cannot reach it (it needs a clip to
         address); double-click-lane is the new verb that can.
         Observed through the lanes BELOW the folder moving, exactly as
         folder_sum_preview.qxa observes a collapse/expand.
         ================================================================ -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 7: folder, no clips -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 8: (temp) child -->
    <add-sample trackIndex="8" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <reparent-track source="8" destParent="7" destIndex="-1"/>
    <!-- Top level: 0,1,2,3,4(collapsed folder),5,6,7(folder w/ 1 child). -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>                         <!-- 8: sentinel -->

    <collapse-track trackPath="7" collapsed="0"/>

    <!-- Expanded: rows 0..6 one each, 7=folder's own lane, 8=its child,
         9=sentinel. -->
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="7" collapsed="0"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="8" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=9"/>

    <!-- The bare lane click: no clip under the pointer on track 7 at all. -->
    <double-click-lane trackPath="7" time="0"/>

    <!-- Collapsed: the child row is gone, sentinel moves up to row 8. -->
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="7" collapsed="1"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="8" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=8"/>

    <!-- A second bare-lane double-click re-expands it (a genuine TOGGLE,
         unlike rule 2's take lanes — the fold triangle itself toggles). -->
    <double-click-lane trackPath="7" time="0"/>
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="7" collapsed="0"/>
    <assert-lane-overlay trackPath="8" grabWidth="900" grabHeight="1200" expectOverlay="true" minPixels="0" contains="row=9"/>
    <double-click-lane trackPath="7" time="0"/>
    <assert-lane-view trackPath="7" collapsed="1"/>

    <!-- ================================================================
         NEGATIVE CONTROL: a bare lane on a PLAIN (non-folder) track's own
         row does nothing — no fold to toggle, no tab, no new track. Track
         0 has a 4 s clip at [0,192000); double-click well past its end,
         still inside track 0's own lane.
         ================================================================ -->
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="9"/>
    <double-click-lane trackPath="0" time="480000"/>
    <assert-track-count arrangement="" count="9"/>
    <assert-tab-set names="Arrangement,Drums,Drums2" active="Arrangement"/>
  </actions>

</SActionScript>
